<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rennes Cathedral</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a> Cathedral, dedicated to Saint Peter, is the cathedral of Rennes, capital of Brittany. The dukes of Brittany were crowned there in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, the Dauphin Francis being the last in 1532. The present neoclassical building was largely rebuilt between 1787 and 1844 after the old church collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rennes-cathedral.jpg" alt="Rennes Cathedral" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pymouss · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rennes_-_Cath%C3%A9drale_Saint-Pierre_120908-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A cathedral has stood on the site more or less since the see was founded in the 6th century. The first building was replaced in the 12th century by a Gothic cathedral, whose tower and entire west front collapsed in 1490. The present façade, with its neoclassical granite towers in four stages, took about two centuries to build, with long pauses: the lowest level dates from 1541 to 1543, the second from 1640 to 1654 under Tugal Caris, and the fourth from 1654 to 1678 under Pierre Corbineau. François Hoguet completed the towers between 1679 and 1704 at their present height of 48 metres.</p><p>In this cathedral, on 25 December 1483, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>, the future Henry VII of England, then in exile in Brittany, promised to marry Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> in order to rally the army that had stayed loyal to the late Edward IV. Their marriage in 1486 sealed the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The nave and choir had never been restored. During vespers on 11 February 1754 a great stone fell from the choir roof, and it was decided to demolish both before the whole body of the church fell. The demolition, carried out between 1756 and 1768, left only the towers and west front. Rebuilding began in 1787 but was suspended during the French Revolution and resumed only in 1816 under Mathurin Crucy; after his death in 1826 the local architect Louis Richelot carried it on. While the cathedral was unusable, from 1803 the abbey church of Notre-Dame-en-Saint-Melaine, named after Saint Melaine, traditionally the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, served as a pro-cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The interior was first decorated in a plain neoclassical style, but during the 19th century it was refurbished with gilding, stucco and paintings inspired by those of the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>. The rich decoration, which includes slabs of rare marble from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a> before the altar, a gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, contrasts with the sombre granite of the structure. The cathedral is the seat of the Archbishop of Rennes, Dol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a>, and has been a monument historique since 1906.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rennes Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes minted their own gold and silver coins, were crowned in <b>Rennes Cathedral</b> and upheld a written customary law, the Très ancienne coutume de Bretagne, compiled in the early fourteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
